---
smd: 2
title: Inline directives in spec v2
summary: Pins how spec v2 reads inline directives. Any :name[content]{attrs} after whitespace, opening punctuation or the line start is a directive, and a name outside the vocabulary renders its content in a plain span. Names are read in any case. unknown-directives.smd pins the same text read with spec v1.
status: draft
updated: 2026-10-17
---

## Names outside the vocabulary

Read :abbr[HTML] as a word, and :term[**bold** text]{#glossary-term .pill color=blue} with a style.

A name without content, with only attributes: :sep{.wide}.

Content that runs over a line break: :abbr[Hyper
Text Markup Language].

## Names in any case

Ship :Badge[Shipped]{color=green} and :STATUS[On track]{color=green}, press :Kbd[Ctrl+S], and see :REF[fig-flow].

- [ ] Write the release notes :Due[2026-11-01] :Priority[P1] :Mention[@dana]

:::figure{#fig-flow} The reading flow
The version comes first.
:::

## A near miss

:badg[beta]{color=green} is read as an unknown directive, with a warning and a fix to `:badge`.

## Not directives

- Escaped: \:abbr[HTML] stays text.
- After a letter or a digit: 10:30 and note:[x] stay text.
- In code: `:code[x]`.
